ult of mostly republicans the  second drag is afghanistan  so if kovid improves by november of 2022  for the midterms  i believe people will have forgotten  about afghanistan in general foreign  policy impacts voting less than domestic  policy and biden himself is not actually  on the ballot you can see november of 22  as a sort of referendum on joe biden for  sure but this may not really be the end  of the world for joe biden and democrats  in november of 22. now for comparison to  prior presidents joe biden's approval  today  is still seven points higher than  trump's at this point in their  presidencies  but below both where barack obama was at  this point in his presidency  and below where george w bush's approval  was  at this point in his presidency  so what does it mean what's next really  there's no change from what we outlined  last week on afghanistan execution aside  joe biden got us out of a war that we  needed to get out of that's good that's  the big story the big story is  bush got us into afghanistan  um  obama didn't get us out  trump didn't get us out  biden got us out were there execution  mistakes of course and we've talked  about it there's no doubt about that but  i don't believe that the big narrative  which is biden got us out  and it didn't go perfectly is going to  impact 2022  the tough part comes where biden now  really needs a big success  but the lower his approval rating the  less likely that republicans are going  to be incentivized to work with him  unless he makes some gigantic  compromises for example  on infrastructure which might turn  biden's victory into a pyrrhic one a  pyrrhic victory one in which you sustain  so much damage in getting the victory  that in practice it's a question as to  whether really it's a victory so it's  this vicious circle biden needs a big  accomplishment to buoy his approval  rating but having a lower lower approval  rating reduces the pressure on  republicans to actually work with joe  biden because 